From ebfd897c897cbfdafdca5898dcbc009865e321de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vili Kohonen <vili.kohonen@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 14:35:01 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Stop a run once it has converged to writing harmful graders

Every run that finds the hack spends its tail at a saturated fixed point: ~100% of
rollouts tamper with the grader, honest solving is zero, most groups have no reward
spread, and the held-out honest pass rate is flat from step ~80 on. Training that tail
costs 30-70% of the bill and buys refinement of the hack, which is not the thing under
study. This adds an opt-in rule that ends the run once the fraction of rollouts labelled
is_reward_hack_loose holds at or above a threshold for a sustained number of batches.

Calibrated on the six existing 200-step runs: at 0.95 sustained 5 steps the rule fires
at step ~59/87/90/100/138 on the five runs that hacked and never fires on the one that
did not, and after a sustained crossing no run ever dipped back below 0.95. A 0.99
threshold is noisy (three runs dip back under it), 0.90 barely fires earlier.

Loose, not strict, because strict also requires the solution to be wrong and so moves
with residual coding ability; loose is the tampering event itself.

Mechanics: the check runs at reward time for step N from reward_extra_infos_dict's
is_reward_hack_loose flags (exact, per-rollout, no wandb involved). The trigger lowers
the trainer's total_training_steps so step N+1 is the last, and the ordinary
is_last_step path does the final checkpoint save, adapter archive and loop exit. The
workers built their LR schedules from the original max_steps at init and are untouched,
so a stopped run's steps are a prefix of the full-length run under the same seed, not a
reshaped schedule -- unlike passing a smaller --steps, which re-stretches the cosine.

Configured via two GRPOConfig fields (early_stop_loose_rh_frac, 0 disables;
early_stop_sustain_steps, default 5), rendered into an `early_stop` block of the verl
config the same way the recontextualization block travels. main_run_rl reads
RLRH_EARLY_STOP_LOOSE_FRAC / RLRH_EARLY_STOP_SUSTAIN from the environment so every
entrypoint gets the knob without widening its signature; the values are resolved in the
driver before Ray starts, so no worker needs the variables.

Error labels (-1.0) count in the denominator and never as hits, so evaluation failures
delay the stop rather than hasten it. An arm whose rewards carry no loose flag warns
once and never fires.

tests/test_early_stop.py pins the decision logic batch by batch; it loads the module by
file path because src.train.__init__ imports torch, and it has been executed (8 passed)
on a machine with no training venv. The trainer glue is not covered, same as the other
trainer patches.
